+## Proposed security incident collector — Stage A, review pending
+
+This section is a proposal for [#2138](https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/issues/2138), not deployed/accepted operation. Code review will be linked from #2138 when the Stage A PR is opened. The PR retains the source-compatibility snapshot, synthetic fixtures and offline lifecycle tests under `ops/security-collector/` and `scripts/security_collector/`. Approval of this proposal does not enable ingestion, publication or closure.
+
+Read-only checks on 23 November 2026 confirmed chart 1.42.0, node-agent v0.3.310 on metal6, storage v0.0.382 and the repository-pinned scanner/operator images. Only the local stdout exporter is configured. Despite its name, its JSON goes to container **stderr**. The retained R1000 record's field layout matches the pinned exporter and armotypes; the [accepted single-worker canary](https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/issues/2136#issuecomment-7087742825) remains the functional evidence. No HTTP/webhook/hosted event pipeline was demonstrated. Native HTTP export exists in the pinned source but is not configured or qualified here.
+
+Vulnerability evidence is available through the namespaced aggregated storage API `spdx.softwarecomposition.kubescape.io/v1beta1`: `vulnerabilitymanifests`, `vulnerabilitymanifestsummaries` and `sbomsyfts`. Posture summaries remain under `workloadconfigurationscansummaries` and are not another issue source in this stage. The existing recurring runner checks report time, SBOM identity, deployed digest, workload summary attribution, DB lock, generation and latest attempt/coverage. Assessment health means freshness/integrity, not absence of vulnerabilities. Current field-name checks are compatibility evidence only, not a fresh acceptance of all workloads.
+
+### Proposed bounded architecture and remaining gates
+
+Use one isolated collector writer with a private SQLite volume, a separately authenticated private ingestion adapter, and a separately controlled public projection. A prospective runtime adapter must qualify native HTTP single/bulk serialization, authenticated sender identity, bounded retries, durable cursors/spool and gap/heartbeat reporting on the exact pinned version. A Kubernetes-log reader is an alternative requiring explicit log RBAC and restart/rotation qualification; a successful `kubectl logs` read alone is not durable delivery. Stage A only implements the file adapter interface. No receiver, service, token, RBAC or Flux manifests are enabled.
+
+A future read-only assessment adapter must join recurring qualified history/DB lock to report/SBOM/summary and independently reread enrolled workload/container digest and generation. It must propagate failed, stale, missing, deliberately excluded and unsupported coverage. Report-body assertions alone cannot certify completeness or deployment. Do not submit scans, change schedules or inherit old-digest success. Non-CVE/GHSA advisory families need a reviewed mapping before use. Exploitability/baseline-aware prioritization remains a separate review gate; the offline default publishes only confirmed High/Critical findings.
+
+Proposed access is limited to enrolled workload/controller inventory and named Kubescape assessment resources; runtime transport gets a dedicated identity/audience and sender allowlist. GitHub credentials, if later approved, use SOPS and a repository-scoped issue-only identity. No access to Gitea or general Secrets is needed. Restrict outbound destinations using approved application/transport controls and verify them independently: Cilium `policyEnforcementMode=never` remains unchanged and cannot be assumed to enforce a new NetworkPolicy. No privileged collector, remediation controller, workload termination, traffic blocking or automatic merges are proposed.
+
+Before enabling ingestion, qualify durable transport/gap handling, inventory/history attribution, volume sizing/backups, bounded resource use, least-privilege access and source authentication. A proposed private raw spool is capped at 100 MiB and 24 hours, with explicit saturation/gap signals and no public export; it is not implemented or accepted in Stage A. SQLite holds normalized state only: at most 200 incident keys, 20,000 seven-day receipts and 32 evidence snapshots per incident. Saturation fails closed and requires reviewed archival, without evicting live replay identities. Initial rate limits are 1,000 observations/hour, 60 GitHub requests/hour, 10 writes/hour, 20 writes/day and three creates/day. Reassess capacity before production enablement; these are defaults, not measured requirements.
+
+Public output requires an explicit reviewed workload mapping and typed field allowlist. Only rule/CVE/GHSA IDs, severity, digest, canonical timestamps/counts/state, fixed investigation text and grounded same-repository issue references are eligible. Raw process/event bodies, arguments, environment, package paths, source URLs/remediation text and private Gitea evidence stay private. Human issue notes outside the collector markers are preserved. Production GitHub integration still needs complete lookup including closed issues, writer ownership checks, optimistic concurrency and lost-response qualification. An uncertain create is fenced until an existing issue is recovered or a reviewed operator investigation proves its outcome; absence from search never permits blind resubmission.
+
+### Lifecycle, ownership and rollback proposal
+
+Runtime deduplication uses source/rule/logical workload/container; CVEs share a workload/container image backlog across digests. Changed digest/generation requires reassessment. Stopped events never close incidents. Runtime acknowledgement stays unresolved; a trusted documented disposition can resolve, and a new detection reopens. Vulnerability recovery requires a qualified clean result for the current deployed digest/generation, a healthy latest attempt, complete coverage, report age at most 72 hours, DB age at most seven days and independent inventory at most one hour old. Failed/stale/missing evidence invalidates assessment-based recovery. Following a telemetry gap, replay alone does not restore resolution. Public writes and closure each default disabled; Stage A has no production GitHub client.
+
+Gatus owns service health, Kyverno issue sync owns policy findings, this proposed collector owns Kubescape runtime/vulnerability incidents, Renovate/CI proposes and qualifies fixes, and Flux deploys reviewed changes/rollback. A service-health recovery or policy pass cannot close a security incident.
+
+Stage A rollback is to remove/revert this offline library: no running component is changed. The local rollback test disables delivery, retains evidence/state and sends no further writes. For a separately approved future rollout, first disable the writer through GitOps and revoke its issue credential if needed; preserve SQLite, replay receipts, unresolved-create fences, private evidence and existing issues. Stop ingestion with a recorded coverage gap, then revert the collector release through Flux. Do not delete scanner reports/SBOMs/PVCs, clear fences, close issues in bulk or loosen other controls. Restoring the same bounded state and reconciling existing keys must precede re-enablement; actual production rollback remains unqualified.
